Title:
Transportable Detonation Chamber (TDC)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Transportable Detonation Chamber is a pre-configured Windows 11 malware detonation VM and unified web interface for testing samples against multiple EDR/telemetry sources.  
- It solves the setup/automation gap of building a repeatable sandbox that correlates execution (DetonatorAgent), kernel/user telemetry (Fibratus ETW, Sysmon), and rule-based detections (Rustinel Sigma/YARA/IOC) into one timeline and process graph.  
- The project emphasizes real-time ETW-driven visibility, multi-channel Windows Event Log browsing with threat highlighting, and integrated static/dynamic analysis via LitterBox (e.g., PE-Sieve/Moneta/HollowsHunter).  
- It includes a dark-themed Flask Web UI with tabs for tracing, process relationship graphs, Sysmon correlation, scanner tooling (ThreatCheck/DefenderCheck), and PE/ELF binary inspection (entropy, imports, packer heuristics).  
- Useful for red teams and malware developers to quickly validate payload behavior/detections, and for blue teams/purple teams to reproduce and study alerting/telemetry in a controlled lab.  
- Interesting because it combines multiple open-source “EDR-like” components into a portable, automated Windows 11 environment (Hyper-V or macOS Apple Silicon via UTM/QEMU) with an API-driven workflow.

Technical Focus:
- Windows ETW telemetry collection and correlation (kernel + event logs)
- Sigma/YARA/IOC rule matching (Rustinel) with NDJSON alerting
- Automated detonation orchestration (Detonator + DetonatorAgent)
- Sysmon event ingestion, filtering, and Windows Event ID correlation
- Static + dynamic malware analysis pipeline (LitterBox: PE-Sieve, Moneta, HollowsHunter, etc.)
- PE/ELF parsing and heuristic triage (entropy, imports, mitigations, packer detection)

Use Cases:
- Rapid payload detonation to observe process/network/registry/file behaviors and resulting detections
- Regression testing of malware/payload changes against Sigma/YARA/IOC rules and telemetry
- Purple-team exercises to validate logging coverage (Sysmon/ETW channels) and detection logic
- Triage of suspicious binaries with built-in PE/ELF analysis and hex inspection
- Building repeatable malware-analysis labs on Windows hosts (Hyper-V) or macOS Apple Silicon (UTM)

Keywords:
Windows 11, malware detonation, sandbox, ETW, Sysmon, Fibratus, Rustinel, Sigma, YARA, IOC, Detonator, DetonatorAgent, LitterBox, PE-Sieve, Moneta, HollowsHunter, ThreatCheck, DefenderCheck, WinRM, Hyper-V, Vagrant, QEMU, UTM, Flask, process graph, Windows Event Log, AMSI, LOLBins